WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.745
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:06.045 --> 00:00:08.967
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:17.086
Unit twenty-seven: Ethical Consumption. In this unit, you will discuss responsible shopping and corporate honesty.

4
00:00:18.083 --> 00:00:19.410
conscious consumer.

5
00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:28.204
conscious consumer means a shopper who considers the social and environmental impact of purchases.

6
00:00:28.504 --> 00:00:31.467
Conscious consumers check where products are made.

7
00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:35.644
Your turn. Say: conscious consumer.

8
00:00:38.344 --> 00:00:39.670
conscious consumer.

9
00:00:40.683 --> 00:00:41.835
greenwashing.

10
00:00:42.535 --> 00:00:47.240
greenwashing means making false or exaggerated environmental claims.

11
00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.805
Calling plastic bottles 'eco' is greenwashing.

12
00:00:51.505 --> 00:00:53.960
Your turn. Say: greenwashing.

13
00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:57.777
greenwashing.

14
00:00:58.783 --> 00:01:00.214
ethical sourcing.

15
00:01:00.914 --> 00:01:06.571
ethical sourcing means buying materials in ways that respect workers and the environment.

16
00:01:06.871 --> 00:01:09.532
The brand promotes ethical sourcing of cotton.

17
00:01:10.232 --> 00:01:13.407
Your turn. Say: ethical sourcing.

18
00:01:16.107 --> 00:01:17.352
ethical sourcing.

19
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:20.361
supply chain transparency.

20
00:01:21.061 --> 00:01:25.882
supply chain transparency means openness about where and how products are made.

21
00:01:26.182 --> 00:01:30.097
Supply chain transparency reveals hidden labour abuses.

22
00:01:30.797 --> 00:01:34.970
Your turn. Say: supply chain transparency.

23
00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:39.786
supply chain transparency.

24
00:01:40.783 --> 00:01:41.761
boycott.

25
00:01:42.461 --> 00:01:45.761
boycott means refusing to buy something as a protest.

26
00:01:46.061 --> 00:01:48.641
Students organised a boycott of the brand.

27
00:01:49.341 --> 00:01:52.539
Your turn. Say: boycott.

28
00:01:55.239 --> 00:01:56.124
boycott.

29
00:01:57.117 --> 00:01:58.141
fair wage.

30
00:01:58.841 --> 00:02:02.338
fair wage means pay that is enough to live on decently.

31
00:02:02.638 --> 00:02:05.346
Garment workers are demanding a fair wage.

32
00:02:06.046 --> 00:02:09.209
Your turn. Say: fair wage.

33
00:02:11.909 --> 00:02:13.050
fair wage.

34
00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:15.481
product lifecycle.

35
00:02:16.181 --> 00:02:20.769
product lifecycle means all the stages of a product from production to disposal.

36
00:02:21.069 --> 00:02:24.334
A product lifecycle analysis measures total emissions.

37
00:02:25.034 --> 00:02:28.464
Your turn. Say: product lifecycle.

38
00:02:31.164 --> 00:02:32.525
product lifecycle.

39
00:02:33.517 --> 00:02:34.645
upcycling.

40
00:02:35.345 --> 00:02:40.120
upcycling means turning old items into new, more valuable products.

41
00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:43.406
Upcycling turns old saris into bags.

42
00:02:44.106 --> 00:02:46.689
Your turn. Say: upcycling.

43
00:02:49.389 --> 00:02:50.448
upcycling.

44
00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.763
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:54.063 --> 00:02:59.676
Rithy, this shirt claims to be '100% eco'. What do you think?

46
00:03:00.983 --> 00:03:04.518
It might be greenwashing. There's no certification label.

47
00:03:05.817 --> 00:03:09.326
It can't be fully eco if it was shipped across the world.

48
00:03:10.617 --> 00:03:14.767
True. And the workers who made it may not have been paid a fair wage.

49
00:03:16.083 --> 00:03:22.346
Many garment factories are in Cambodia. Supply chain transparency should be the norm.

50
00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:27.588
Some of my friends support a boycott of brands that hide information.

51
00:03:28.883 --> 00:03:32.206
Boycotts must have an effect if enough people join.

52
00:03:33.517 --> 00:03:38.421
Personally, I prefer upcycling. My sister makes bags from old clothes.

53
00:03:39.717 --> 00:03:43.527
Grammar focus: Modals of deduction (extended).

54
00:03:43.827 --> 00:03:46.315
Use modals to make logical deductions.

55
00:03:46.615 --> 00:03:50.472
It might be greenwashing, but it can't be fully eco.

56
00:03:50.772 --> 00:03:53.096
The workers may not have been paid fairly.

57
00:03:53.396 --> 00:03:59.018
Use passive modal perfects (may not have been paid) to speculate about past actions done to people.

58
00:04:00.017 --> 00:04:03.064
Now read and listen. Shopping With a Conscience.

59
00:04:03.364 --> 00:04:06.606
More consumers than ever say they want to shop ethically.

60
00:04:07.917 --> 00:04:12.447
However, it is often difficult to know whether a product's claims are true.

61
00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:20.057
Greenwashing has become so common that some regulators now fine companies for misleading labels.

62
00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:27.146
Ethical sourcing and supply chain transparency allow shoppers to see how goods are made.

63
00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:35.117
Cambodia's garment industry, which employs hundreds of thousands of workers, is under increasing scrutiny.

64
00:04:36.417 --> 00:04:41.922
Campaigners argue that brands must guarantee a fair wage throughout their supply chains.

65
00:04:43.217 --> 00:04:49.175
Some consumers organise boycotts, while others choose second-hand clothes or upcycling.

66
00:04:50.483 --> 00:04:55.327
Every purchase, it is often said, is a vote for the kind of world we want.

67
00:04:56.617 --> 00:04:59.478
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:04:59.778 --> 00:05:03.287
Making false environmental claims is blank.

69
00:05:03.587 --> 00:05:08.192
A, upcycling. B, greenwashing. C, ethical sourcing.

70
00:05:12.183 --> 00:05:14.775
The answer is B: greenwashing.

71
00:05:16.283 --> 00:05:19.423
He isn't answering. He blank be in a meeting.

72
00:05:19.723 --> 00:05:23.237
A, might. B, can't. C, mustn't.

73
00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:29.099
The answer is A: might.

74
00:05:30.583 --> 00:05:33.805
That blank be true. I saw her yesterday.

75
00:05:34.105 --> 00:05:37.491
A, must. B, can't. C, should.

76
00:05:41.483 --> 00:05:43.529
The answer is B: can't.

77
00:05:45.017 --> 00:05:48.642
Refusing to buy something as a protest is a blank.

78
00:05:48.942 --> 00:05:53.942
A, boycott. B, fair wage. C, product lifecycle.

79
00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:00.368
The answer is A: boycott.

80
00:06:01.883 --> 00:06:05.857
According to the text, what have some regulators started doing?

81
00:06:06.157 --> 00:06:13.351
A, banning shopping. B, fining companies for misleading labels. C, closing factories.

82
00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:21.834
The answer is B: fining companies for misleading labels.

83
00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:28.974
Now it's your turn. Part one: Can individual shoppers really change how companies behave?

84
00:06:34.274 --> 00:06:38.910
Part two: Speculate with modals, give two arguments and one example.

85
00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:59.591
They might, if enough of them act together. Consumer pressure can force companies to publish their supply chains, and boycotts may damage reputations. The campaign for garment workers' wages, for example, must have influenced several brands.

86
00:06:59.891 --> 00:07:02.076
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:03.083 --> 00:07:07.860
Great work today! You learned eight new words and modals of deduction (extended).

88
00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:12.357
Today's value is responsibility. Help at home and do your jobs.

89
00:07:12.657 --> 00:07:15.541
See you in Unit twenty-eight. Goodbye!

90
00:07:15.841 --> 00:07:18.517
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

